#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "Most of this data is about children, and that one fact shapes the five ideas below."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "1. Some data is sensitive"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "Not all data needs the same care. A published count of schools in each district harms nobody. A learner's name and address need protecting. A learner's health condition, or a child-protection record, needs the most protection of all, and the law usually says so."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "So we give every table a **sensitivity tier**, on a scale that runs from public statistics to special-category personal data. Everything else follows from that tier: who may see the table, where it's stored, and whether it may leave the platform. You'll find the tiers listed in [terminology]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "To go further, read [CAP-CLASSIFICATION]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "2. Show less than the whole"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "Most people who use EMIS data don't need to know who each person is. A planner needs counts, and an analyst needs patterns. So wherever it can, the platform shows less than the whole record. It has three main ways of doing that:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "**Masking** hides the value in a column from people who don't need it. For example, it might show a birth year instead of a full birth date."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "**Pseudonymisation** replaces a name or a national number with an identifier that nobody can trace back to the person without a key, and that key is protected separately."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "**Aggregation with suppression** publishes counts, but hides any count so small that it could identify someone."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "NIST describes these techniques in [*De-Identifying Government Datasets*]{1} (SP 800-188, 2023)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "To go further, read [CAP-ACCESS]{1} and [CAP-PUBLICATION]{2}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "3. Who can see what: roles and attributes"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "There are two well-known models for deciding who can access what, and an EMIS needs both of them."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "**Role-based access control (RBAC)** grants permissions to roles, and people hold roles. Analysts may run queries, for instance, and stewards may approve access. RBAC answers the question *what kind of thing may this person do?* ([NIST, RBAC]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "**Attribute-based access control (ABAC)** decides from attributes of the person, the data and the request, such as where the person works, how sensitive a column is, and what the request is for. ABAC answers the question *which rows and columns?* ([NIST SP 800-162]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "Here's how they work together. A district officer holds the analyst role, so they may query enrolment; that's RBAC. They're posted to District B, and that posting is an attribute, so they see only District B's learners; that's ABAC. And the health column is masked for them because of its sensitivity tier, which is ABAC again."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "To go further, read [principle P14]{1} and [CAP-ACCESS]{2}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "4. Share by publishing, not by copying"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "An EMIS shares data with other parts of government, with researchers and with the public. But every copy that leaves the platform is a copy that nobody can correct, delete on schedule, or audit. So the blueprint prefers to **publish**. It publishes aggregate statistics openly, and it gives named partners governed access to de-identified data, under an agreement and for a declared purpose. Raw exports of personal data are the exception, and when they happen, they're recorded."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "To go further, read [principle P15]{1} and [CAP-SHARING]{2}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "5. Know where every number came from"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "**Lineage** is the record of where data came from and what was done to it. For example, it might tell you that an indicator was computed from a particular snapshot, which counted certain returns, which in turn were loaded from certain files. Lineage is what lets you:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "trust a published figure, because you can trace it back to its source;"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "find everything that's affected when a source is corrected;"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "delete a person's data everywhere it went, when the law requires it."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "The platform captures lineage automatically as data moves, using the open [OpenLineage]{1} standard. Nobody has to write it down by hand afterwards."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "To go further, read [CAP-LINEAGE]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "Keep in mind"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "As you read the specifications, you'll find the same five questions coming up again and again:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "How sensitive is this data?"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "Does this person need the whole record, or less?"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "Which role, and which attributes, allow this access?"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "Can it be published instead of copied?"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/data.md
msgid "Could we trace this number back to its source?"
msgstr ""

